WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.680
After an eight-year trek, a joint ESA, Jackson Spacecraft, is about to become the third to orbit Mercury and the first to deliver two probes at once. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 172. The ESA and Jackson mission will arrive at the innermost planet following a series of gravity assists that stretch the cruise phase. Two orbiters will separate upon arrival to study the planet's surface, exosphere, and magnetic field from complementary vantage points.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:45.260
A new media kit outlines the final approach timeline and the instruments each spacecraft carries. Scientists expect the dual spacecraft configuration to resolve longstanding questions about how Mercury's unusually strong magnetic field is generated.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:55.000
The mission's arrival media kit details every remaining milestone from the final flybyes through orbital insertion and the start of routine science operations.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:09.540
Beppy Columbus arrival marks the first time two spacecraft will orbit Mercury simultaneously. One will map the surface and magnetic field at low altitude, while the other studies the planet's thin, exosphere from a higher vantage.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:17.100
The dual perspective should finally clarify why Mercury's magnetic field is so much stronger than models predicted for its size.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:31.560
The mission's success also demonstrates how gravity assist tours can deliver complex payloads to difficult destinations without larger rockets. Watch for the first science data releases once the orbiters settle into their operational paths.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:42.660
Now on a completely different note, researchers examine tiny metallic grains inside primitive meteorites to reconstruct magnetic fields present when the sun and planets were forming.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:56.500
The measurements show that magnetic fields in the inner solar nebula were strong enough to influence how dust clumped into larger bodies. The work provides direct evidence of conditions that existed roughly 4.6 billion years ago.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:10.320
The study focuses on grains that recorded field orientations before they were locked into parent asteroids. Follow-up studies will compare grains from different meteorite classes to map how those fields varied with distance from the young sun.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:26.780
Picture a cloud of gas and dust once spanning roughly 100 times the distance from the sun to Neptune. If that entire structure were scaled down to the size of a football stadium, the region where Earth eventually formed would occupy only the space of a single blade of grass.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:36.900
Inside that vast but diffuse disk, tiny metallic grains began aligning with invisible magnetic field lines, like compass needles caught in wet cement.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:49.340
As the grains were later incorporated into asteroids and then broken apart into meteorites, those alignments froze in place, preserving a snapshot of field strength from more than 4 billion years ago.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:03:01.860
Laboratory instruments can now read the orientation of those grains to within a few micro Tesla, revealing that the inner nebula's magnetism was strong enough to help dust overcome turbulence and clump together.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:12.980
Yet we still cannot explain why the field strength appears to drop so sharply beyond the present day asteroid belt, a pattern that shows up consistently across multiple meteorite families.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:21.460
Solving that gradient would tell us whether magnetic fields or some other process set the boundary between the rocky and icy worlds we see today.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:31.200
Engineers at Texas A&amp;M have released a preliminary design for a lunar landing and launch facility intended to support sustained surface operations.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:39.400
The concept includes pads, refueling infrastructure, and navigation aids located 240,000 miles from Earth.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.340
The proposal aims to reduce risks for future landers by providing a standardized arrival point.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:52.440
The news release emphasizes that the facility would serve as a common hub for both robotic and eventual crewed missions.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.040
Development is still in the study phase, with no construction timeline announced.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:09.100
A Soyuz 21b-slash-fragot lifted off from Placetsk at 0240 UTC on August 24, and delivered a spacecraft believed to be Cosmos 2619.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.740
The payload is suspected to support the GLONASS navigation constellation.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:19.760
The launch marked the latest in Russia's ongoing military space program from the Northern Cosmodrome.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:26.280
Orbital tracking confirms the upper stage performed its planned burns to reach the intended navigation orbit.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:33.140
NASA's Perseverance rover captured a silhouette of the Martian moon Phobos passing in front of the solar disk.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:37.300
The event lasted only seconds from the rover's viewpoint on the surface.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:43.620
Such transits help refine orbital models of Phobos and demonstrate the rover's camera timing precision.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.160
The images add to a growing record of Phobos transits observed by surface assets.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:55.040
Scientists plan to use similar observations to monitor changes in the moon's orbit over time.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:05.180
Washington State University researchers trained an algorithm to evaluate millions of possible printing parameter combinations for a high-performance aerospace alloy.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:11.960
The system identified six viable approaches that reduce waste and avoid the need for extensive physical testing.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.540
The method cuts development time for components that must withstand extreme temperatures and stresses during launch.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:25.360
The alloy in question is intended for rocket-grade applications where both strength and heat resistance are critical.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:34.260
By analyzing 75 meteorite falls recorded on video, scientists traced how objects lose mass through melting and fragmentation,

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:42.340
rather than simple evaporation. The data show distinct stages from atmospheric entry to final deceleration and ground impact.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.860
The findings improve models used to predict where future meteorites will land and how much material survives.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:56.000
Researchers identified seven repeatable phases that occur regardless of the meteoroids initial size or composition.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.840
An orbital view highlights the chain of glacial land forms extending into northern lake Huron.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.600
The image reveals sheltered channels and coves shaped by past ice sheets.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:12.100
Such observations help track changes in coastal wetlands and water clarity over time.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:17.820
The view captures both the exposed islands and the intricate network of waterways between them.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:24.260
The author of the Martian and Project Hail Mary will serve as executive producer for exoplanet.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.780
An original audio series scheduled for release this fall.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:35.080
The project continues weir's focus on realistic space exploration scenarios told through character-driven stories.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:42.180
The drama will explore themes consistent with weir's previous novels while reaching listeners through the audio format.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:52.040
A Wahoo caught off the Virginia coast ended up aboard a spacecraft after its odoliff was selected for a microgravity experiment on bone density analogues.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:58.120
The study compares calcium structures in the fish ear bone with human bone loss in space.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:02.560
Results are expected to inform countermeasures for long duration missions.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:09.620
The odoliff selection highlights an unexpected bridge between marine biology and astronaut health research.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:17.300
Four new modules let students examine data from the twin spacecraft still returning measurements from beyond the heliopause.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:25.180
The materials cover particle counts, magnetic field readings, and the ongoing power management decisions keeping the probes alive.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:29.900
Educators can access the resources through the heliophysics resource database.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:34.840
The modules are designed for grades 6 through 12 and higher education settings.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:43.480
A new worksheet challenges grades 6 through 12 learners to convert one light day into familiar units using Voyager 1's current physician.

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:48.420
The exercise connects basic arithmetic to real spacecraft tracking data.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.220
Solutions guide users through the scale of interstellar distances.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:58.840
The problem set uses the probes actual trajectory to illustrate how far the spacecraft has traveled since launch.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:08.640
Satellite and field data show that eroding wetlands from Texas to Maine deliver more than 660,000 metric tons of carbon to the ocean each year.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.700
The research links storm frequency and sea level rise to accelerated marsh loss.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:17.080
Findings will feed into regional carbon cycle models.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:24.600
The study combines NASA Earth-observing assets with ground measurements along the entire U.S. Atlantic and Gulf coasts.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:33.560
Two recent papers trace how the young sun's intense flaring and the solar systems motion through the galaxy may have influenced Earth's early atmosphere.

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:41.600
The studies combine stellar observations with geological records to identify possible drivers of past climate shifts.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:46.060
Researchers will test the models against additional exoplanet systems.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:54.560
The work suggests that both internal solar activity and the sun's galactic orbit played roles in shaping long-term climate conditions on Earth.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:01.800
Keep an eye on the first science data releases from Beppy Colombo once the orbiters settle into their operational paths.

